R API for Naomi app

App to show district level estimates of HIV indicators

## Running in docker

Docker images are built on GitHub actions, if on main branch run via:

```
docker run --rm -d --network=host --name hintr_redis redis
docker run --rm -d --network=host --mount type=volume,src=upload_volume,dst=/uploads \
  -e USE_MOCK_MODEL=true --name hintr ghcr.io/hivtools/hintr:main
```

For a more complete example of running on a network see [docker test script](https://github.com/hivtools/hintr/blob/main/docker/test).

HINT_EXAMPLES

Docker container can be cleaned up using
```
docker rm -f hintr hintr_redis
```

### Input data

Input data should be written to the shared `upload_volume`. When requesting validation pass the absolute path to the file in the request JSON e.g.

```
{
  "type": "pjnz",
  "path": "/uploads/Botswana.pjnz"
}
```

## Validating JSON against schema

To turn on validation of requests and responses you need to set the environmental variable VALIDATE_JSON_SCHEMAS to true. You can do that by writing to a `.Renviron` file, on linux `echo -e "VALIDATE_JSON_SCHEMAS=true" >> .Renviron`.


## Tracing (OpenTelemetry)

The API can send an OpenTelemetry trace span for each request. It uses the [otel](https://otel.r-lib.org) and [otelsdk](https://otelsdk.r-lib.org) packages. Tracing is off unless you choose an exporter. When it is off, it costs one check per request.

Each span has:

* the method and the route template, e.g. `GET /model/status/<id>`. A request that matches no route is named by its method only
* the status code. A 5xx marks the span as an error
* the trace of the caller, if the request has a W3C `traceparent` header. hint sends one, so hintr's span sits under hint's span in the same trace

Spans do not have the path (it has ids and file names), the query string, the headers or the bodies.

Some packages that hintr uses make spans of their own, e.g. DBI for the duckdb queries. They are children of the request span. DBI's spans have the table and the operation, not the query. To keep only hintr's spans, set `OTEL_R_EMIT_SCOPES=hintr`.

The rrq workers (model fits, calibration, downloads) are not traced. The request that submits a job has its span like any other.

Turn it on with the standard environment variables:

| Variable | Value | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `OTEL_TRACES_EXPORTER` | `otlp` (or `console` to print spans) | Chooses the exporter. Unset: no tracing |
| `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` | e.g. `http://localhost:4318` | Where to send. `/v1/traces` is added to it |
| `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` | `hintr` | The name of the service. The docker image sets it to `hintr`. Without it, otel uses `R` |
| `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES` | e.g. `deployment.environment=production` | Optional |

otelsdk only sends OTLP over HTTP (`http/protobuf`, the default, or `http/json`), not gRPC. So the endpoint must be a collector's OTLP/HTTP port, usually 4318, not its gRPC port 4317. If `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL` is set to `grpc`, override it with `http/protobuf`.

otelsdk is in Suggests, as it needs C++ to build. The docker image has it. To try it locally, install it with `install.packages("otelsdk")`, then e.g.

```
OTEL_TRACES_EXPORTER=console OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=hintr Rscript -e 'hintr:::main_api()'
```

See `?otelsdk::environmentvariables` for the other settings.

## Running tests

To run tests locally:

1. Install all dependencies with `devtools::install_deps(".")`. You may be prompted to install some operating system
    packages; these should be available via your package manager but for `protoc` you may need the following instructions:
   https://askubuntu.com/questions/1072683/how-can-i-install-protoc-on-ubuntu-16-04
1. Some packages need to be installed from GitHub:
    * `devtools::install_github("ropensci/jsonvalidate")`
    * `devtools::install_github("mrc-ide/eppasm")`
    * `devtools::install_github("mrc-ide/naomi")`
    * `devtools::install_github("mrc-ide/rrq")`
1. Install the hintr package:
   ```
   R CMD INSTALL .
   ```
1. If running all tests, including those that require redis, start a redis docker container
    ```
    docker run --rm -d --network=host --name hintr_redis redis
    ```

Finally tests can be run with `devtools::test()`.

## Using sensitive data

To run tests which use sensitive data you need to clone the private [naomi-data repo](https://github.com/mrc-ide/naomi-data) into `tests/testthat/testdata/sensitive`.

```
git clone git@github.com:mrc-ide/naomi-data.git tests/testthat/testdata/sensitive
```

## Running commands against a deployed environment

Sometimes you need to run R interactively against a live deployment - checking
configuration, running a one-off maintenance script, or just debugging.

### Getting a shell

```bash
az containerapp exec -g <resource-group> -n <container-app-name> --command R
```

This opens an interactive R session inside the running container. Use `--command bash` instead if you want a plain shell rather than R directly.

Resource group and app names are environment-specific - check the relevant
deploy repo's bicep/parameter files, or list the apps in the resource group
with `az containerapp list -g <resource-group>`. For the main naomi
production deployment, the API container app is `nm-hintr` in `nmHint-RG`:

```bash
az containerapp exec -g nmHint-RG -n nm-hintr --command R
```

### Running things once you're in

From an R session you can connect to the queue the same way the API itself does:

```r
queue <- hintr:::Queue$new(workers = 0)
```

That gives you a live `rrq` controller (`queue$controller`), so you can call any `rrq`/`hintr` function directly, inspect Redis state, or run a one-off script.

## Adding prerun model results

Details here will depend on the deploy (and that will be the place to look for the running version).

Use `hintr::prerun_push`, specifying the *relative* filenames of the output, spectrum and summary files.

First, run a model using `naomi::hintr_run_model` into some directory, say `mydir`

Then import the data into the *production copy of naomi* with

```
hintr::prerun_push("mydir",
                   output = "malawi_output.qs2",
                   spectrum = "malawi_spectrum_download.zip",
                   summary = "malawi_summary_download.zip")
```

Make a note of the hash that is returned - you'll need that if you want to delete the data.

**You must be on the VPN for this to work.**
